module lib/hf/molecule
  The `Molecule` record every Hartree-Fock routine of lib/hf reads, and the
  defs that build it and answer simple questions about it. A molecule here
  is the nuclei (charge and position of each atom) plus the basis: the
  Gaussian shells placed on each atom, whose functions the orbitals are
  built from. Both are kept as the numeric packs the integral code indexes,
  laid out as PySCF lays out its own,
  so lib/hf can be compared with PySCF pack for pack.

molecule_bohr  @b4bfaefa
  why   Every other way of making a molecule (from angstrom, from text) ends
        here, so the order of shells and the numbering of basis functions are
        decided in one place. That order is PySCF's: atoms in the order
        given, each atom's shells in the table's order.
  z       the atomic number of each atom, `[natm]`
  xyz     each atom's position in bohr, `[natm, 3]`
  b       the basis set table (lib/basis/basis), shells listed per element
  natm    how many atoms
  atoms   the atoms pack, `[natm, AT_LEN]`: charge and position of each atom
  shells  the shell pack, `[nsh, SH_LEN]`, one row appended per shell: its
          atom, angular momentum `l`, number of primitives, first primitive
          row, and the index of its first Cartesian and first spherical basis
          function
  prims   the primitive pack, `[npr, PR_LEN]`, one row appended per Gaussian:
          its exponent and contraction coefficient
  cart0   the index the next shell's first Cartesian function gets
  sph0    the index the next shell's first spherical function gets
  a       the atom being placed
  s       the table row being read
  l       that shell's angular momentum
  np      that shell's number of primitives
  sh      the shell's row of the shell pack
  k       one of the shell's primitives
  pr      that primitive's row of the primitive pack

  pack   fill the atoms, shells and primitives packs, numbering the basis functions as each shell is placed, and stop if an element has no shells in the basis
  return the neutral molecule, spin from the electron count's parity, spherical functions

molecule_from  @e0372cb7
  why   Angstrom is PySCF's default unit and the unit people write
        geometries in. Dividing by `MOL_BOHR` the way PySCF does (multiplying
        by its inverse) keeps the positions equal to PySCF's to the last bit.
  z    the atomic number of each atom
  xyz  each atom's position in angstrom, `[natm, 3]`
  b    the basis set table

molecule_packs  @dd55a75b
  why   The lock ladder holds lib/hf against packs PySCF wrote out
        (lib/hfref); wrapping them as a `Molecule` lets those runs use the
        same entry points as a molecule built here.
  atoms   an atoms pack made elsewhere
  shells  a shell pack made elsewhere
  prims   a primitive pack made elsewhere
  n       the sum of the nuclear charges, which fixes the spin's parity

mol_nelectron  @0bef7974
  why   The nuclear charges and the net charge are what the record stores;
        the electron count is derived, so the two cannot disagree.
  mol  the molecule

mol_nalpha  @78506ddf
  why   The SCF fills orbitals per spin, so it needs the two counts. A spin
        that does not match the electron count's parity has no such split,
        and the run stops instead of rounding.
  mol  the molecule
  n    the number of electrons

mol_nbeta  @98200f62
  mol  the molecule

mol_enuc  @ee1ea9e7
  why   The nuclear repulsion is a constant added to the electronic energy
        to give the total energy a Hartree-Fock run reports.
  mol    the molecule
  atoms  its atoms pack: charge and position of each atom, in bohr
  e      the repulsion summed over the pairs seen so far, in hartree
  a      the first atom of a pair
  b      the second atom of a pair, one before `a`, so each pair counts once
  dx     the pair's separation along x, in bohr
  dy     the pair's separation along y
  dz     the pair's separation along z

mol_lmax  @ceaf84ae
  why   It decides whether any shell has spherical functions that differ from
        the Cartesian ones (mol_spherical).
  mol  the molecule

mol_spherical  @916d8b74
  why   The integrals are computed over Cartesian functions; only when this
        is `1` does the SCF transform them to spherical ones, so a basis with
        no d shells skips the transform.
  mol  the molecule

mol_nao  @3cafcce1
  why   The size of the SCF's matrices (overlap, Fock, density), for a
        program that needs it before a run. In the repository only this
        module's tests call it.
  mol  the molecule
